From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius Iversen <marius.iversen@elastic.co>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 16:49:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] virtio/vsock: deliver what a unix proxy's host wrote before
 it closed

When the host end of a stream mapped to a unix socket closes, the socket
hangs up. process_event() took the HANG_UP arm first, reset the guest's
connection and never read what the host had written just before closing.
A socket also hangs up when the guest has shut down its send direction and
the host then shuts down its own, so a request and response exchange in
which the guest half-closes first lost the tail of the response. With one
megabyte written and closed at once, the guest received about 890 kB.

A hang-up on a connected proxy is now handled like readable data: the
proxy reads until recv() returns end of file and only then ends the
connection. The macOS epoll emulation reports end of file as IN, so it
takes the same path.

End of file without a hang-up means the host only stopped sending. The
guest is now told so with VSOCK_OP_SHUTDOWN and can keep sending, where it
was reset before. The connection is reset when the guest stops sending
too, or when a later send to the host socket fails.
---
 src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/muxer.rs     |   6 ++
 src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/muxer_rxq.rs |  17 ++++
 src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/unix.rs      | 104 +++++++++++++++++++---
 3 files changed, 113 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

